use WordFlowerNavigator from @nav
use Local from @tao/data
use HarnessNavigator, StackByLet, StackByLetWith, StackByNav, StackByNavWith from ./

// Tranche status: absorbed
//
// SURFACES AND DECLARATIONS. This tranche gives WordFlower the UI primitives, adaptive layout,
// declaration model, and minimal design system used by the code in this directory.
//
// The contract is now a DIRECTORY. Every mapped source and test file here is part of it:
//
//   WordFlower       this entry file — project, apps, navigation, local datasource, variants, shell
//   Design           the minimal design system — implemented last, see that file
//   @ui/             the workspace, document, and focus-session areas
//   @nav/            the navigation values and the deterministic harness navigator
//   @data/           the data catalog
//   *.test           the behavior journeys, each beside what it exercises
//
// Declarations carry the narrowest visibility that works. A file-private declaration carries no
// marker; `folder` reaches the rest of this directory with no `use` line, which is why the sibling
// import lists are gone. Entities, apps, and case sets still resolve through their own paths, so
// those keep an explicit `use`. `ui` and `nav` are separate folders, so what they publish across a
// boundary is `workspace` and is imported by path.
//
// Each tranche's scratch contracts graduate before its boundary: the Prelude and `@tao/text` to
// `packages/stdlib/@tao` and
// `packages/stdlib/@tao/text`, and this tranche's `duration` primitive and `@tao/time` to the
// Prelude and `packages/stdlib/@tao/time`. Their `@tao-next/` copies are intentionally absent at
// this absorbed boundary.
//
// The absorption gates must compare `1 - Current` and `2 - Next` as whole directories: the same
// file set, with every file byte-identical to its counterpart when the tranche closes. Implementing
// directory gating was part of this tranche. The repeated sidecar markers were bootstrap input and
// were removed when the directory contract gained one status.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - Stdlib surfaces: `ScrollView`, `Image`, `Checkbox`, `Spinner`, and `Progress`. Selectable
//   collection rendering stays language-owned: `loop Plural / Singular { ... }` scopes its template
//   block and optional `on select` to the row, so no stdlib `List` or function-typed row slot is
//   introduced
// - Text stdlib: `@tao/text` exports the injected functions `CountWords(Value is text)` and
//   `Join(Values is list of text, Separator is text)` with the exact behavior specified by
//   `packages/stdlib/tao/text/Text.tao`
// - Adaptive layout: `Panes()` lays its children side by side when, after gaps, each child can
//   receive at least 320 logical pixels; otherwise it stacks them in source order. In horizontal
//   mode, `claim` distributes remaining width proportionally. `width max <n>` bounds a readable
//   column. This adaptation is fixed stdlib container behavior, not language breakpoint syntax
// - `async { }` runs its block without delaying the statements after it; failures surface as
//   provider error state
// - Primitive value heads make the common product declaration explicit: `nav N = StackNav { ... }`,
//   `datasource S = Local { ... }`, and `app X = Y with { ... }` declare values, while the rarer
//   reusable type form is `type T is nav|datasource|app with { ... }`. A direct `nav N { ... }`
//   instantiates primitive `nav`; it fills only members `nav` already declares. It never declares a
//   new nav type or adds members, and it is usable only when those fills plus primitive defaults make
//   the value complete. `let` remains the universal auto-typed immutable binding for local,
//   supporting, computed, and copied values; a primitive head constrains the value family without
//   widening away the more precise inferred type
// - `with` is THE core refinement operation underlying type derivation, type instantiation, and
//   value derivation. It may be omitted only for construction from a base resolved as a type:
//   `Type { <fills> }` is sugar for `Type with { <fills> }`. Derivation from an existing value must
//   keep it visible as `Value with { <delta> }`; `Value { ... }` is not legal. If type and value
//   namespaces both contain a name, bare `Name { ... }` resolves the type namespace and constructs
//   a value. In a value expression, `Name with { ... }` resolves the value namespace first and the
//   type namespace only when no value exists; after `type T is`, the base resolves only the type
//   namespace. The delta always narrows or fills and never reopens a filled member. A value may fill
//   or override declared data/configuration properties but may never introduce a member
//   No `required` keyword is introduced: a supplied property without a default remains required
// - Every app value is declared in the entry file, which keeps cross-module variant derivation
//   unreachable. `run`, imports, and strict app identity accept any complete app value uniformly,
//   whether a primitive head or an inferred `let` declared it; no AST-kind union leaks into semantics
// - A render site composes named clause bundles and direct clauses in one list, and the last
//   specification of a given clause wins: `[screen, claim 2, width max 720]` reads left to right.
//   After same-clause replacement, validation rejects a resolved clause set whose remaining clauses
//   are semantically incompatible; source order never makes incompatible clause categories valid
// - Rendering and presentability are orthogonal core roles. `visual` is their common rendering
//   root; `presentable` refines it, and `ui` and `nav` refine `presentable`. `view`, `layout`, and
//   `frame` refine `visual` without becoming presentable. Content acceptance belongs intrinsically
//   to the declaration kind, not to a property or slot: `view` is a leaf, while `layout` and `frame`
//   accept caller content and place it with `@@content`. A TypeScript-backed container therefore
//   declares itself a `layout`. A layout clause is ambient on every visual occurrence, so nothing
//   declares it and no user name collides with it. `packages/stdlib/tao/Prelude.tao` pins this
//   hierarchy
// - A frame declares an optional named content slot with `@name = empty`. The name is in the frame's
//   slot namespace, `empty` contributes no rendered node, and a caller fills it at most once with
//   `@name <visual>`. A filled slot is opaque visual content placed exactly where `@name` occurs in
//   the frame render body; `Card` and its `@actions` call site exercise the complete contract
// - An injection has no compiler-prefixed names. `TR`, `RN`, and `process` are always in scope in
//   injected TypeScript; the inject list explicitly names every additional Tao-provided binding.
//   Each entry is `<Name> <value>` over any expression, and a bare name binds a property under its
//   own name:
//   `render inject Total, Suffix " words", Content @@content, Layout @@layout, Tag @@tag`.
//   This retires the implicit `_ViewProps` bag and is implemented across Tao, stdlib included. The
//   ambient channels are exactly `@@content`, `@@layout` — which carries the parent
//   direction the occurrence lays out against — and `@@tag`; navigation, dialogue, app, and caller
//   inheritance stay private and never reach a hand-written implementation
// - The value of `render inject` is the view's root element and Tao wraps nothing around it, which
//   is already the emitted behavior. Applying `Layout` and `Tag`, and rendering `Content` exactly
//   once, are the implementation's responsibility and are not checked
// - Every `ui`, `view`, `layout`, `frame`, `dialogue`, `action`, and `function` declaration has a
//   parenthesized parameter list, including `()` when empty, mirroring its call sites:
//   `view WordCountLabel(Value text)`, `action SaveDocument()`, `ui Settings()`. Omitting the list
//   is invalid. A `function` has a block body and hands its result back with `return`, so early
//   returns are available; `returns <type>` is optional and inferred when omitted
// - `let X is Type = value` ascribes a binding: `is` narrows, `=` binds. It keeps aligned `let`
//   columns and preserves `Name is Type` order rather than inverting to type-first
// - List types always name their element type as `list of T`; bare `list` is invalid in type
//   declarations and parameter ascriptions
// - `type Name is one of Cases` declares a nominal type in the ordinary type system, and each case is a
//   value of that type. One-case enums are valid; `ConfirmResult` deliberately uses `none` for
//   dismissal rather than declaring a second case
// - `optional <Name> <type>` declares an item field that may be absent
// - `expect checkbox <selector> checked|unchecked` joins the test vocabulary, since a two-state
//   control cannot be asserted through visible text
// - Minimal design: flat tokens plus named clause bundles applied at render sites, and an app
//   `Design` property. The lexer replaces `TAG` with one shared terminal,
//   `TagOrHexColor: /#[A-Za-z0-9_]+/`. AST context distinguishes tag and design-token positions.
//   The validator requires tags to match `#[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` and design color values to match
//   CSS hexadecimal `#RGB`, `#RGBA`, `#RRGGBB`, or `#RRGGBBAA`, case-insensitively
//
// FOCUSED WRITING. This tranche gives WordFlower a focused writing session — pick a length, write
// until the clock runs out, pause and resume, or stop early — and with it the first capability cut
// from the Current ↔ MVP gap: unit values (Decisions §2) and the ticking clock (§9), reached
// through the expression-position TypeScript boundary (§15) and proved under a controllable test
// clock (§16).
//
// Earlier tranches' decisions are recorded in `Docs/Spec/` and `Docs/Roadmap/Tao Revolution/Decisions.md`;
// this header lists only what this tranche decides:
// - Unit values (§2): `.unit` on a number constructs a typed value and `.unit` on a typed value
//   reads it back as a number in that unit, so `10.min`, `1.s`, and `220.ms` construct durations
//   and `Wait.s` reads one. A value belongs to one family with a canonical base and fixed ratios.
//   The mechanism is generic; `duration` — `ms`, `s`, `min`, `h`, `d`, `wk`, with §2's long singular
//   and plural aliases — is the only family this tranche registers. Equality normalizes
//   (`60.s == 1.min`); a cross-family read is a compile error; arithmetic is dimensional: duration
//   ± duration → duration, duration × number → duration, duration ÷ duration → number, and anything
//   else is a compile error, except that a unit value compares against the bare literal `0`
// - Calendar arithmetic (§2): `now` is an ordinary expression anywhere, not only a `time` default;
//   `time − time → duration` and `time + duration → time`
// - `duration` is a primitive type in the Prelude, used wherever a type goes: a parameter
//   `Length duration`, a `returns duration`, a `state` or `let` holding one
// - `.Clock` reads a duration as clock text: whole seconds, `m:ss` under an hour and `h:mm:ss` from
//   an hour up, and `0:00` for zero or negative
// - `@tao/time` (§9): `use Interval from @tao/time` imports its declarations by name, like every
//   other package, and `Interval(Every)` constructs a reactive ticker that starts when the view
//   holding it mounts and stops when that view unmounts. `Tick.Value` is the time as of the latest
//   tick, `Tick.Running` whether it ticks, and `do Tick.Stop()` and `do Tick.Start()` control it.
//   No clock grammar enters the language. This amends Decisions §9 and §11, which sketched a
//   lowercase service import (`use time from @tao/time`, `time.Interval(1.s)`): a package binding
//   would need a second `use` form and a package-as-namespace value, and the segment names of the
//   packages that already exist — `ui`, `nav`, `data` — are Tao keywords, so the shape does not
//   generalize to the packages it would first apply to
// - A `let` over a ticker is a live derivation: `let Left = EndsAt - Tick.Value` recomputes on
//   every tick, and every display that reads it re-renders
// - `<expression> from <path>` (§15) in expression position, binding loosest: the expression is a
//   name or a call to one, that head name is the sidecar's named export, and any arguments are
//   ordinary Tao expressions. An arbitrary expression to the left would leave every name in it
//   ambiguous between Tao scope and module scope; §15's own examples all name the export as the
//   head. This is how `@tao/time` binds its runtime. Value-position `inject <type> … ```ts` retires
//   with it — `@tao/text` and `BuildStamp` migrate — while `render inject` stays as the view
//   authoring mode
// - The compact two-outcome `when <boolean> <Yes> / not <No>` (§8) is the value form a display of
//   `Left` needs: `when Left > 0 Left.Clock / not "Time's up"`
// - A toast's `Duration` is a duration: `present … as toast (Key: "…", Duration: 3.s)`
// - Tests (§16): every test starts from a fresh, fixed clock, and `advance <duration>` moves it,
//   firing every interval tick that falls due, in order, so a ticking journey is deterministic.
//   Toast expiry is thereby testable and is proved in the documents journey
project {
   name "WordFlower"
   remote none
   license MIT
}

// An app mounts navs, it does not render content directly. Overlay and toast hosting are universal
// capabilities, so no app declares them.
app WordFlower {
   Name "WordFlower"
   Design WordFlowerDesign
   Navigator WordFlowerNavigator
   Datasource DeviceStore // offline-first: the device owns the writing; sync is a variant
}

app WordFlowerDrawer = WordFlower with {
   Name "WordFlower - Drawer Preview"
   Navigator with { // patches the app's own navigator instead of replacing it
      Display "drawer"
}  }

app WordFlowerNavHarness {
   Name "WordFlower Navigation Harness"
   Navigator HarnessNavigator
   // An app without data omits `Datasource` entirely
}

// These four apps cross the two independent declaration sugars. Each is a complete app value with
// the same mounted behavior: primitive head versus inferred `let`, and canonical construction versus
// explicit `with`. The Foundation test sidecar runs all four, so every spelling must compile and work.
app WordFlowerStackByNav {
   Name "WordFlower Declaration Head"
   Navigator StackByNav
}

app WordFlowerStackByNavWith = app with {
   Name "WordFlower Declaration Head With"
   Navigator StackByNavWith
}

// A configured datasource is a named value like any other. The bare constructor block is the
// canonical surface spelling for the same refinement operation that explicit `with` exposes;
// deriving from an existing datasource keeps `with` visible.
datasource DeviceStore = Local {
   StorageKey "WordFlowerData"
}

folder
let WordFlowerStackByLet = app {
   Name "WordFlower Declaration Let"
   Navigator StackByLet
}

folder
let WordFlowerStackByLetWith = app with {
   Name "WordFlower Declaration Let With"
   Navigator StackByLetWith
}
